and a man may be one step closer to  finding his father who drowned decades  ago thanks for joining us here at six  i'm denise faldiz i'm kirsten joyce the  clark county coroner now narrowing down  the estimated age for a set of remains  found at lake mead the 8 news now  i-team's david charn speaking to a man  who's now more confident those bones  could be his father david yeah this set  of bones discovered in calville bay back  in may it's not the murder victim  founded that barrel but the remains of  somebody who investigators say likely  drowned many years ago as we first told  you just a couple of weeks ago here todd  kolodd believes that person could be his  father daniel kola drowned in the same  area in 1958 there's his photo he was  just 22 and a half years old the  coroner's office today saying those  remains are likely from a person who was  between 23 and 38 when they died while  his father was six months shy of that  cut off todd kolod says this is just the  latest bit of information that points to  his dad he talked to me from overseas  today with each clue i always expect in  my mind that it's going to put put us  farther away from our goal  but consistently  each clue is putting us closer  and this is like a bull's-eye  a bull's-eye he says well the i-team  showed the photos of the remains to a  dentist who said she believes the person  was missing several front teeth and  likely wore a partial denture now that's  important because daniel kolod was in a  car crash a few years before his death  and he wore a partial denture  a spokesperson for the county says the  coroner is attempting to identify these  remains in the other two sets also  discovered at the lake and they're doing  this all through dna and that could take  years though this new piece of  information is providing this family  just a little more hope tonight i'm  david charns live local now
 

